- Archaeologist Nadia Karimova excavated a ninth-century trading post at Bulgar, a medieval Volga Bulgarian settlement in present-day Tatarstan, Russia.
- She recovered 214 glass beads whose chemical composition matches glassworks in Fustat, the administrative capital of Fatimid Egypt.
- She also found 53 carved walrus-ivory combs consistent with Norse craftsmanship from Scandinavia.
- Conventional scholarship describes Volga Bulgaria as a regional power trading primarily with neighboring Khazar and Slavic communities.
- Karimova argues that the artifact assemblage indicates Bulgar participated in a transcontinental exchange network extending from Egypt to the Norse world.

The student wants to contrast the conventional scholarly view of Volga Bulgaria''s trade with Karimova''s findings in order to underscore the broader geographic reach her evidence reveals. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', E'- Archaeologist Nadia Karimova excavated a ninth-century trading post at Bulgar, a medieval Volga Bulgarian settlement in present-day Tatarstan, Russia.
- She recovered 214 glass beads whose chemical composition matches glassworks in Fustat, the administrative capital of Fatimid Egypt.
- She also found 53 carved walrus-ivory combs consistent with Norse craftsmanship from Scandinavia.
- Conventional scholarship describes Volga Bulgaria as a regional power trading primarily with neighboring Khazar and Slavic communities.
- Karimova argues that the artifact assemblage indicates Bulgar participated in a transcontinental exchange network extending from Egypt to the Norse world.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"At the Bulgar site in Tatarstan, Karimova unearthed 214 glass beads from Fatimid Egypt and 53 Norse walrus-ivory combs, artifacts that span a geographic range far exceeding the neighboring Khazar and Slavic trade partners that conventional scholarship assigns to Volga Bulgaria."},{"key":"B","text":"Karimova's excavation at Bulgar yielded glass beads chemically traced to Fustat and carved walrus-ivory combs of Norse origin, demonstrating that the site contained luxury goods from multiple distant regions."},{"key":"C","text":"Conventional scholarship characterizes Volga Bulgaria as a regional power whose trade connections were limited to nearby Khazar and Slavic communities rather than extending across the continent."},{"key":"D","text":"Archaeologist Nadia Karimova excavated a ninth-century trading post at Bulgar, where she recovered hundreds of artifacts including glass beads and ivory combs that she believes indicate long-distance trade."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The goal requires both contrasting the conventional view with Karimova''s findings and underscoring the broader geographic reach. The option beginning "At the Bulgar site" names the specific artifacts from Egypt and Scandinavia, then explicitly juxtaposes their transcontinental origins with the conventional view''s limited Khazar and Slavic trade partners, fulfilling both parts of the rhetorical goal. The option describing beads from Fustat and Norse combs presents Karimova''s evidence without referencing the conventional view, so it achieves no contrast. The option summarizing conventional scholarship states only the existing view without introducing Karimova''s counterevidence, leaving the contrast incomplete. The option introducing Karimova''s excavation generically mentions artifacts and long-distance trade without specifying the origins or contrasting them with prior scholarly assumptions.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-09-28"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-09-28","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);


- Engineer Amara Osei designed a low-cost water filtration membrane using cellulose extracted from plantain peels.
- Laboratory tests showed the membrane removed 97 percent of lead particles and 93 percent of arsenic from contaminated groundwater samples.
- Conventional polymer-based filtration membranes cost approximately \\$42 per square meter to produce.
- Osei''s plantain-cellulose membrane costs roughly \\$7 per square meter, using agricultural waste readily available in Ghana and Nigeria.
- The membrane degrades within 18 months under continuous use and must be replaced more frequently than polymer alternatives.

The student wants to present Osei''s membrane as a promising but imperfect solution for communities with contaminated groundwater. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', E'- Engineer Amara Osei designed a low-cost water filtration membrane using cellulose extracted from plantain peels.
- Laboratory tests showed the membrane removed 97 percent of lead particles and 93 percent of arsenic from contaminated groundwater samples.
- Conventional polymer-based filtration membranes cost approximately \\$42 per square meter to produce.
- Osei''s plantain-cellulose membrane costs roughly \\$7 per square meter, using agricultural waste readily available in Ghana and Nigeria.
- The membrane degrades within 18 months under continuous use and must be replaced more frequently than polymer alternatives.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Osei's plantain-cellulose membrane filters 97 percent of lead and 93 percent of arsenic at just \\$7 per square meter, yet its 18-month lifespan means communities must replace it far more often than conventional polymer filters."},{"key":"B","text":"Plantain peels, an agricultural waste product common in Ghana and Nigeria, can be processed into cellulose and formed into filtration membranes for water purification."},{"key":"C","text":"Conventional polymer filtration membranes cost about \\$42 per square meter, whereas Osei's plantain-cellulose alternative costs only \\$7 per square meter to produce."},{"key":"D","text":"Engineer Amara Osei has developed a membrane that removes lead and arsenic from groundwater, addressing a critical public health challenge in West African communities."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The goal requires presenting both promise and limitation. The option citing filtration rates, low cost, and the 18-month lifespan directly establishes the membrane as effective and affordable (promising) while noting the frequent replacement requirement (imperfect). The option about plantain peel processing describes raw material sourcing without addressing either the membrane''s effectiveness or its drawbacks. The option comparing costs highlights only the price advantage without mentioning filtration performance or limitations. The option about removing lead and arsenic presents only the public health benefit without acknowledging the membrane''s durability shortcoming.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"pr